WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:14.614
Charles Darwin’s father once told his son, "You will be a disgrace to yourself and all your family," because Darwin, an apathetic student, seemed only interested in dogs, hunting, and catching rats.

2
00:00:14.615 --> 00:00:22.725
But at 22, when he had the opportunity to join an expedition on the ship Beagle , Darwin’s life

3
00:00:22.726 --> 00:00:32.935
changed. One result of that trip was On the Origin of Species , a landmark book published in 1859 that described the theory

4
00:00:32.936 --> 00:00:49.919
of evolution. It was a revolutionary idea, maintaining that each species of every plant and animal gradually evolves from common ancestors and, over time, develops traits that help it respond to environmental pressures and enables it

5
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.529
to survive. Modern scientists consider this theory one of the most important scientific advances

6
00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:04.548
in history. Darwin was born February 12, 1809, in England, to a prominent physician named

7
00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:09.631
Robert Darwin. His mother died when Darwin, the fifth of six children, was

8
00:01:09.632 --> 00:01:23.360
only eight. His father, now the central figure in his life, was dismayed that his son was not interested in school, preferring long, solitary walks observing nature and collecting insects, leaves,

9
00:01:23.361 --> 00:01:29.565
and rocks. Later, Darwin attended medical school at Edinburgh University to please

10
00:01:29.566 --> 00:01:39.717
his father. But young Darwin was aghast at the barbarity of the surgery performed there and thought the lectures were "intolerably dull," so his grades

11
00:01:39.718 --> 00:01:49.545
suffered. Disappointed again by his son’s academic indifference, Dr. Darwin enrolled his son at Cambridge University, hoping he would become a

12
00:01:49.546 --> 00:01:57.095
clergyman. But at Cambridge, Darwin serendipitously met the most important mentor of his life and chose another

13
00:01:57.096 --> 00:02:13.644
path. In the early nineteenth century, collecting beetles became a craze. College students competed to see who could find a new species of beetle, and young men went on nature walks hoping to best their fellow collectors by finding rare

14
00:02:13.645 --> 00:02:29.183
specimens. Darwin needed some expert advice to beat his rival, Charles "Beetles" Babington, so he went to John Stevens Henslow, a professor of botany. Thus began a relationship between the two men that lasted a

15
00:02:29.184 --> 00:02:48.210
lifetime. It was Henslow who encouraged Darwin to join the Beagle expedition even though Darwin’s father was against it, calling the trip "a wild scheme." So on December 27, 1831, Charles Darwin set out on a five-year, round-the-world sea

16
00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:59.160
voyage. Although he suffered from acute seasickness in the beginning, the trip was just what Darwin needed, and he explored and collected specimens wherever the ship

17
00:02:59.161 --> 00:03:14.151
anchored. Most of the trip was spent mapping the South American coastline; although he didn’t know it at the time, the five-week stopover in the Galápagos Islands, off the coast of Ecuador, would prove to be a pivotal moment in his scientific

18
00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:27.237
thinking. There, Darwin collected, dissected, preserved, and labeled specimens from all branches of zoology and made notes on their environment, appearance, and even their stomach

19
00:03:27.238 --> 00:03:37.295
contents. When he returned to England in 1836, Darwin carried with him about 3,000 pages of notes and about 5,000

20
00:03:37.296 --> 00:03:42.913
specimens. Soon Darwin began to ask himself questions about what he had found in South

21
00:03:42.914 --> 00:03:54.122
America. Why, for example, did members of one species isolated on an island have different characteristics from a similar species on the continent or on another

22
00:03:54.123 --> 00:04:03.537
island? As was popular at the time, Darwin joined scholarly groups such as the Zoological Society and the Geological Society of

23
00:04:03.538 --> 00:04:11.102
London. As a member, Darwin frequently attended lectures and presented papers to enthusiastic

24
00:04:11.103 --> 00:04:20.261
audiences. In addition, societies were meeting places where he could discuss his scientific findings and, more importantly, his many

25
00:04:20.262 --> 00:04:30.136
questions. To Darwin, it seemed the most important piece of the evolutionary puzzle concerned the bird species he had collected on the Galápagos

26
00:04:30.137 --> 00:04:41.022
Islands. Birds that Darwin thought were grosbeaks, wrens, and orioles were in fact 13 different species of the same kind of bird—a finch.

27
00:04:41.023 --> 00:04:52.557
These finches had various types of beaks: some were short and stubby while others were long and pointy. Other beak types ranged between these two extremes.

28
00:04:52.558 --> 00:05:00.170
Darwin observed that the type of beak seemed to vary according to the type of food available where the finches lived.

29
00:05:00.171 --> 00:05:11.259
Darwin’s theory of evolution started taking shape after he began considering these differences, and he even drew a sketch of an evolutionary tree in his notebook.

30
00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:27.103
Basically, the theory states that of the offspring produced by a species, only those suited to find food, mates, and nesting places in their environment will survive to genetically pass on their traits to the next generation.

31
00:05:27.104 --> 00:05:36.422
In an environment where the only food is hard seeds, only birds with beaks that can crush such seeds are likely to survive.

32
00:05:36.423 --> 00:05:53.248
Evolution takes place as traits are passed down through each generation; eventually, members of one species will gradually change until a new species develops. Darwin called this process "evolution through natural selection."

33
00:05:53.249 --> 00:06:02.663
In 1838, Darwin began to write out his ideas, but lacking confidence in his own credentials as a scientist, did not publish them.

34
00:06:02.664 --> 00:06:16.613
Darwin worried about how his church and his family would react to his new theory, which challenged religious beliefs in divine creation. His wife, Emma, the mother of his ten children, was a devout Christian.

35
00:06:16.614 --> 00:06:32.417
Darwin eventually put snippets of his theory into a book, The Voyage of the Beagle , published in 1839, and in l844, he outlined his theory in a manuscript he later circulated among a few

36
00:06:32.418 --> 00:06:54.590
friends. At the same time, Darwin worked to develop his scientific expertise, publishing on various topics in natural history and corresponding with scientists such as botanist Joseph Dalton Hooker, geologist Charles Lyell, and Alfred Russel Wallace, a young, self-educated biologist like

37
00:06:54.591 --> 00:07:11.507
himself. When Darwin learned in 1858 that Wallace was planning to publish his own paper on natural selection, he realized that (after working far longer on the concept than Wallace) he was about to lose the glory of being the first to announce the

38
00:07:11.508 --> 00:07:27.554
theory. So Charles Lyell suggested the two men jointly present papers on natural selection in July 1858 at a meeting of the Linnean Society, a natural history society. But the papers created little

39
00:07:27.555 --> 00:07:39.924
excitement. When Darwin published On the Origin of Species the next year, however, the book proved to be unexpectedly popular. But a storm of criticism arose too, as Darwin had feared.

40
00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:46.416
Some of his friends and colleagues defended him and his theory, but others bitterly attacked him.

41
00:07:46.417 --> 00:07:57.912
In 1871, his book The Descent of Man claimed that people were just another species of animal and that humans and apes evolved from a common ancestor.

42
00:07:57.913 --> 00:08:11.744
Cartoons of Darwin with a long beard and an ape body suddenly appeared in the press, but Darwin stood by his scientific findings; worrying about public opinion never seemed to be part of his temperament.

43
00:08:11.745 --> 00:08:21.239
Darwin is considered the father of modern biology because he recognized the common evolutionary thread that all living things share.

44
00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:37.113
His work included 19 books and many scholarly papers, which spanned subjects as diverse as barnacles, coral, the relationship between plants and their pollinators, and the role of earthworms in creating mold.

45
00:08:37.114 --> 00:08:45.160
Darwin attributed his achievements to his "methodical habits" and his "complex and diversified mental qualities."

46
00:08:45.161 --> 00:08:55.687
He wrote, "Of these the most important have been—the love of science—unbounded patience . . . and a fair share of invention and commonsense."

47
00:08:55.688 --> 00:09:08.811
He died April 19, 1882, and was buried at Westminster Abbey close to Sir Isaac Newton, the man who discovered gravity. Darwin’s father would finally have been proud.

